      "writtenOn": "2020-10-14T20:54:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Also, not you, but once we suspend the port, I don\u0027t see where we actually drain the queue with the old and now invalid messages? I see this done for TCPMv1 in the suspend state, but I don\u0027t see the parallel in TCPMv2.",
      "revId": "1f619e4a02bd4299878708c31d6f24d3b9d726d4",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "89e40ed3_1a7457dc",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 1193,
      "author": {
        "id": 1269501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-10-24T06:08:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Please see b/156138479 for how this manifests on (TCPMv2 + TCPMv1). Despite belief, no draining occurs in TCPMv1. Rather -- local RX buffer may be serviced, **but remote TCPC RX buffer is still enqueueing messages while being ignored**.\n\nThese remote messages are then all handled at once when I2C is reconnencted (seconds) later, resulting in massive logjarm/corruption/desync.\n\n```\nPort resumes...\nBut we\u0027re handling STALE vdm:attn messages from before the d/c...?\n```\n\nWe\u0027ve long since D/C\u0027ed due to ramifications from \"hanging up\" on the port. We abruptly ceased communication with the TCPC, without turning off SOP\u0027 response. So the queue starts to (inappropriately) handle after the resume.\n\nI can repeat this test on TCPMv2 if it would help. Or on different TCPC with different remote RX buffering behavior. I suspect similar misbehavior exists.",
      "parentUuid": "68f85f9c_08831b35",
      "revId": "1f619e4a02bd4299878708c31d6f24d3b9d726d4",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d6a13e13_0dd169b3",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 1193,
      "author": {
        "id": 1149894
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-23T18:19:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "TCPMv1 does drain the queue. See the snippet from TCPMv1 below:\n\"\n                        rstatus \u003d tcpm_release(port);\n                        if (rstatus !\u003d 0 \u0026\u0026 rstatus !\u003d EC_ERROR_UNIMPLEMENTED)\n                                tcpc_prints(\"release failed!\", port);\n#endif\n                        /* Drain any outstanding software message queues. */\n                        tcpm_clear_pending_messages(port);\n\n                        /* Wait for resume */\n                        while (pd[port].task_state \u003d\u003d PD_STATE_SUSPENDED) {\n\"\nWe should add the same to TCPMv2. Something like the following:\n\nstatic void tc_disabled_entry(const int port)\n{\n    print_current_state(port);\n\n    /* Drain any outstanding software message queues. */\n    tcpm_clear_pending_messages(port);\n}",
      "parentUuid": "89e40ed3_1a7457dc",
      "revId": "1f619e4a02bd4299878708c31d6f24d3b9d726d4",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a0dc8894_fa85c788",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 1193,
      "author": {
        "id": 1269501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-24T20:40:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Adding on, is there a way to flush TCPC FIFO? Do we even have a command for that?\n\nPer b/156138479 we are handling stale `VDM:Attention` messages after \"resume\". Resulting in internal HPD line state corruption in some instances.\n\n[Rhetorical] What would happen if we attempted handling a stale `SRC_CAP` message from TCPC FIFO, but for some reason the partner PDO object numbers changed between connection attempts? Say, 9v Variable Voltage PDO#2 was replaced by 20v PDO#2?\n\nTCPC FIFO should probably be flushed as well.",
      "parentUuid": "d6a13e13_0dd169b3",
      "revId": "1f619e4a02bd4299878708c31d6f24d3b9d726d4",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "13041ebe_4e9c2749",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 1193,
      "author": {
        "id": 1149894
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-24T21:27:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If a \"Flush TCPC FIFO\" command exists, I\u0027d expect it to be documented in the TCPCI spec. Or we could modify tcpm_clear_pending_messages to read the tcpc\u0027s fifo until it\u0027s empty.",
      "parentUuid": "a0dc8894_fa85c788",
      "revId": "1f619e4a02bd4299878708c31d6f24d3b9d726d4",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8ff84f90_d3f88871",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 1193,
      "author": {
        "id": 1269501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-24T22:22:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Could not find documentation in TCPCI spec. \"FIFO\" is only mentioned twice.\n\nFurthermore, it seems TCPC may not be honoring some requirements from TCPCI 2.0v1.\n\n\u003e 4.7.4 Receiving SOP* USB PD Messages with Less than or Equal to 128 Data Bytes\n\u003e \n\u003e  If an overflow has occurred, the TCPC will have set the\n\u003e ALERT.RxBufferOverflow register, therefore the TCPC will not send the GoodCRC to\n\u003e other received messages until the TCPM clears the Message Received alert.\n\u003e \n\u003e The TCPM should always clear the Rx Buffer Overflow and Message Received bits at the\n\u003e same time. Otherwise there could be a scenario where the Rx Buffer Overflow bit\n\u003e remains set even though the TCPM has just cleared one of the messages in the buffer. \n\n(1) I do not see any mention at all of TCPC_REG_ALERT_RX_BUF_OVF beyond the tcpci.h header file. Or handling/clearing of `ALERT.RxBufferOverflow` in our EC codebase. This seems to be an architectural deficiency unrelated to this CL.\n\n[go/cs \"TCPC_REG_ALERT_RX_BUF_OVF\" ec + \"TCPC_ALERT_RX_BUF_OVF\" kernel]\n\n(2) I do not see any method in Vendor-Proprietary TCPC datasheets on how to clear this internal FIFO. Another architectural deficiency.\n\nThe suggested method for \"port suspend\" should involve: (a) disabling SOP* message reception via RxDetect register (b) setting CC Hi-Z Disabled state [yes, killing connection] (c) flushing the message queue manually or resetting the TCPC [as necessary] (d) turning off interrupt handler on EC pin for TCPC [and hope it\u0027s not shared].\n\nCurrently we do not do (a) (b) (c) or (d). That is beyond the scope of this emergent CL.",
      "parentUuid": "13041ebe_4e9c2749",
      "revId": "1f619e4a02bd4299878708c31d6f24d3b9d726d4",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
